What does the final line log, and what is the main trade-off of declaring `handleClick` this way?

```javascript
class Button {
  label = "OK";
  handleClick = () => console.log(this.label);
  render() { return this.label; }
}

const a = new Button();
const b = new Button();
console.log(a.handleClick === b.handleClick, a.render === b.render);
```

What does this code log, and when?

```javascript
const slow = new Promise((resolve) =>
  setTimeout(() => { console.log("slow done"); resolve("S"); }, 1000)
);
const fail = Promise.reject(new Error("boom"));

Promise.all([slow, fail])
  .then((values) => console.log("values", values))
  .catch((err) => console.log("caught", err.message));
```

What does this code log, and what is the most robust built-in way to get an independent copy of `original`?

```javascript
